This will succeed at loading a cert from any supported file format that was sandwiched within `-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----`/`-----END CERTIFICATE-----`.

It's probably worth adding onto CertificatePal a method to only load the X.509 DER format.

For Windows the implementation is essentially

```C#
unsafe
{
    fixed (byte* spanPtr = span)
    {
        CRYPTOAPI_BLOB certBlob = new CRYPTOAPI_BLOB(
            span.Length,
            spanPtr);

        bool success = Interop.crypt32.CryptQueryObject(
            CertQueryObjectType.CERT_QUERY_OBJECT_BLOB,
            &certBlob,
            ExpectedContentTypeFlags.CERT_QUERY_CONTENT_FLAG_CERT,
            ExpectedFormatTypeFlags.CERT_QUERY_FORMAT_FLAG_BINARY,
            0,
            out _,
            out _,
            out _,
            IntPtr.Zero,
            IntPtr.Zero,
            out SafeCertContextHandle target);

        if (!success || target.IsInvalid)
        {
            target.Dispose();

            int hr = Marshal.GetHRForLastWin32Error();
            throw hr.ToCryptographicException();
        }

        return new CertificatePal(target);
    }
}
```

(which might require adding another overload to the CryptQueryObject P/Invokes)
